# The oracle references, pinned.
#
# One line per reference, tab-separated: <name> <image-ref> <version> <what it
# answers>. The last field is prose, which is why the separator is a tab.
#
# **This file is the other half of `tools/corpus/VERSIONS`, and the two pin
# opposite directions.** That file pins the *corpora* - which commit of
# test262, which release of perl's `re_tests` - and it is honest about pinning
# them to whatever Debian 13 gives: "from the release that matches the
# pcre2test on this machine", "the ref matches the glibc on this machine".
# This file pins the *reference that answers*, so that the two can be moved
# deliberately and together. A corpus imported from one version and answered
# by another measures the gap between them and reports it as a defect here.
#
# Two kinds of pin and they are not the same guarantee. A stock image is pinned by digest and the digest is the whole
# guarantee: a tag can be moved under you and a digest cannot, so a green run
# names a set of bytes rather than a set of names. An image **built here**
# cannot be - two builds of one Dockerfile are not two copies of one image -
# so what is pinned instead is everything the build reads, and the run-time
# version check is the real guarantee.
#
# The version field is what the reference must say it is when asked, and it
# names **what decides an answer** rather than what the package is called.
# For a reference carrying bundled Unicode tables that is the data version:
# `tools/corpus/VERSIONS` records that vim, CPython and node were in daily use
# here with no pinned record at all, answering 177,580 rows a run between
# them, and that a reference vendoring its own tables carries a third version
# independent of both this library's and the standard's.
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate, and is its own commit. A new reference version
# is new answers, and a new answer that disagrees is a finding to triage
# rather than a regression to fix - so containerising a reference and raising
# it are two changes, and doing both at once means the next disagreement
# cannot be attributed to either.

# perl, for the Perl-family front end and for four tables it is the only
# reference to. `GRX_SYNTAX_PERL` means "what perl does" rather than what a
# document says, so this is a definition rather than a second opinion.
#
# **5.44.0, raised from 5.40.1 on 2026-09-25 together with the corpus pin in
# tools/corpus/VERSIONS**, which is the only way either can move: importing
# `t/re/re_tests` from one release and answering it with another measures the
# gap between them.
#
# Debian 13 ships 5.40.1 and only one perl can be installed, which is why
# this raise was impossible before there was a file deciding which perl
# answers. It carries UCD 17.0.0, an exact match with tools/unicode/
# UCD_VERSION, and every count it changed is an exclusion that stopped being
# needed - 46 boundary rows, 110 fold code points, 111 numeric ones, and part
# of the script-run unsettled bucket. Zero new disagreements. VERSIONS has
# the measurements.
#
# **There was a `perl-next` pin here and the raise consumed it.** It existed
# so the second perl could be asked the first one's questions before anyone
# committed to it; the reading was taken, and a second pin with nothing left
# to read is weight. The mechanism it proved is still in use - see
# `python-next`, which is the same shape for a decision not yet taken.
perl	docker.io/library/perl:5.44.0-slim@sha256:76d5a0d6f19a3841668e44a67ecb680fc8cd8e3fac4388dd01f48750031bb524	perl v5.44.0	Perl-family match and syntax, case-fold orbits, Numeric_Value, script runs, splitting and iteration

# node, for the ECMAScript dialect - and, because V8 carries ICU, for whole
# Unicode property tables. `check-oracle-properties` compares every property's
# code point set against it, which is only meaningful because node 22.23.2's
# ICU 78.2 is Unicode 17.0, an exact match with tools/unicode/UCD_VERSION.
# That is the reading the version field below is for: CPython's `re` cannot be
# asked the same question, because its UCD is two releases behind.
#
# **The pin names V8, not node**, for the reason node_runner.py exists. V8
# interprets a regular expression and compiles it after a few executions, and
# the two paths do not always agree:
#
#   /(?:(?=a)a)*\B../u  over "\nabc" from offset 1
#
# is 1-4 on the first execution in a fresh process and no match on every one
# after it. `--regexp-interpret-all` gives 1-4 always, which is the answer
# pcre2test 10.46 and perl 5.40.1 agree with. Present in 12.4.254.21 and gone
# by Chrome 153's V8, so it is a fact about this V8 rather than about node -
# and the flag is applied by node_runner.py, which refuses to run without it.
# `make check-oracle-determinism` asserts that it is doing what it is for.
#
# 22.23.2 because that is the node tools/corpus/VERSIONS records and the one
# every count in tests/data/vectors/ecmascript/ was taken against. Raising it
# is its own commit with its own triage.
node	docker.io/library/node:24-slim@sha256:0e0ff40c39bc087845bfb27465a0df4ea419520094bc35842ff83dd8cbe6f9b6	V8 13.6.233.17-node.53, Unicode 17.0	The ECMAScript dialect, and every Unicode property table

# vim, for the Vim dialect and for two whole-plane tables. **Built here: there
# is no official vim image**, which makes it the other kind of pin. What is pinned is everything the build reads -
# the base by digest, vim's source by tag - and the run-time check below is
# the real guarantee.
#
# **The version field names the release and the patch level**, because
# neither identifies a vim on its own. The patch line is what distinguishes
# two builds of one release, and it is four lines down from the useless one:
#
#   9.1 image       Included patches: 1-1244
#   Debian 13's     Included patches: 1-948, 950-1230, 1242, 1244
#
# Contiguous against gappy, which is also the argument for building rather
# than installing: `apt-get install vim` drifts silently on the next security
# update, and a version-pinned apt install eventually fails to resolve. A tag
# has no expiry.
#
# The release line was dead weight for two years - every vim built in that
# time says `VIM - Vi IMproved 9.1` - and this probe read the patch line
# alone because of it. Raising to 9.2 showed why that was only half a
# version: **9.2.1129 prints `Included patches: 1-1129`, a lower number than
# 9.1.1244's `1-1244`**, so the patch line alone reads as a downgrade and
# names no release at all. The probe prints both now.
#
# Why the tables matter here and not elsewhere: vim's character tables are its
# own, in src/mbyte.c, and it publishes no UCD version for them. That is why
# check-vim-classes and check-vim-widths exist - they compare 1.1M code points
# against vim itself rather than against a number it does not state - and it
# is why "which vim" is the whole question for them.
# **9.2.1129, raised from 9.1.1244 on 2026-09-25 together with the table it
# moves.** The raise was measured a day before it was taken and deliberately
# held, because it is not a pin bump: what 9.2 changes is data this library
# committed, so the pin and `src/unicode/vim_class.c` had to move in one
# commit or the gate would have been red in between.
#
# What the raise costs, measured against 9.1.1244 before and after:
#
#   check-vim-widths     1,112,062 code points, **0 disagreements** both ways
#   check-oracle-vim     66,580 rows, unchanged
#   check-vim-classes    **48 code points move**, and they are one block:
#                        U+2070..U+209F, Superscripts and Subscripts, which
#                        9.1 puts in class 1 (punctuation) and 9.2 gives a
#                        class of its own - `charclass()` returns the code
#                        point itself for those, the way it does for CJK.
#
# So the Vim dialect's `\<`, `\>` and `\k` changed meaning for those 48 code
# points, which is what following a reference means. documentation/
# dialects.md carries the row.
#
# Rebuild it with:
#
#   make oracle-images
#
# which reads the tag from this line, or by hand:
#
#   docker build --build-arg VIM_TAG=v9.2.1129 \
#       -t localhost/ghoti-regex-oracle-vim:9.2.1129 tools/oracle/containers/vim
vim	localhost/ghoti-regex-oracle-vim:9.2.1129	vim 9.2, Included patches: 1-1129	The Vim dialect, its nine character classes and its cell widths

# pcre2, for the PCRE dialect - and, alone, for the newline conventions, the
# callout trace and the search window, which nothing else can answer.
#
# **Built here, and what it removes is the argument for it.** On this machine
# pcre2 was reached through three workarounds, all of them in the Makefile and
# all of them there because Debian installs the runtime without the -dev
# package: `pcre2.h` was manufactured out of the pinned release's
# `pcre2.h.in` by substituting four version macros, the `.so` symlink `-l`
# wants was found by scraping `ldconfig -p` with a multiarch guess behind it,
# and `vectors-pcre` skipped for want of `pcre2test`. In the image all three
# are one `apt-get install libpcre2-dev pcre2-utils`, the header is upstream's
# rather than a reconstruction, and the driver links `-lpcre2-8` the ordinary
# way.
#
# The driver is compiled **inside** the image at run time rather than shipped
# in it, the way `chron` compiles its ICU driver: the source lives here and
# the image is deliberately ignorant of this library. What it holds is a pcre2
# and a compiler, and what it builds links only pcre2 - which is the property
# that stops an oracle reaching the implementation it answers for.
#
# The apt version is written out in full in the Dockerfile so that the archive
# moving past 10.46 *fails the build* rather than quietly installing 10.47 and
# reporting a corpus imported from 10.46 as regressed. When that first breaks,
# snapshot.debian.org is the durable answer; it is not worth the weight
# before then.
pcre2	localhost/ghoti-regex-oracle-pcre2:10.46	PCRE2 version 10.46	The PCRE dialect, the newline conventions, the callout trace and the search window

