# whichever CPython ran the tool. tools/oracle/python_match.py puts `re`
# behind the same batch protocol every other oracle here speaks, and the
# speed argument that defended the old shape does not survive measurement -
# what makes a differential slow is a process per *case*, not a process.
# Measured over 120,000 cases: 1.16s in-process, 0.97s host subprocess, 1.89s
# in a container, all three agreeing on all 120,000 answers.
#
# **The version field names the UCD as well as the release**, because `re`
# reads `unicodedata`'s tables and this library's are 17.0.0. The gap has
# teeth and the measurement is in tools/corpus/VERSIONS: **5,650 code points
# have a different general category between 15.1.0 and 17.0.0**, and
# python_diff's safety from that is a property of its subject list - 18
# distinct characters, none of them one of the 5,650 - rather than of the
# comparison. An added subject can lose it with nothing saying so, which is
# why python_diff.py prints both versions on every run.
#
# **3.14.7, raised from 3.13.5 on 2026-09-25 together with the two rules it
# changed.** The raise was held back when the reference was containerised,
# because a newer CPython is new `re` behaviour *and* a newer UCD at once and
# that is two readings in one commit. Both were then taken separately:
#
#   `\z`   accepted as the preferred spelling of `\Z`, and refused inside a
#          class exactly as `\A`, `\B` and `\Z` are. 148 rows.
#   `\B`   matches at position 0 of an empty subject, which is what perl,
#          pcre2 and V8 have always answered and what this library answers in
#          every other dialect. 17 rows here, 37 of check-oracle-split, 1 of
#          check-oracle-replace.
#
# The UCD half moved 15.1.0 -> 16.0.0 and changed no answer: the differential
# ran 111,000 rows against 3.14.7 with 0 disagreements outside those two
