# I-Regexp, RFC 9485, and this is the first reference here that is not an
# implementation of the dialect it answers for - because there is no such
# thing. The dialect is a *format* defined by an ABNF, so two references
# answer its two halves, and both live in one image built here
# (containers/iregexp/Dockerfile).
#
# **iregexp-check answers the syntax half**: is this string an I-Regexp? A
# checking implementation is defined by what it refuses (RFC 9485 section
# 3.1), which is what this library claims to be, so the reference that can
# contradict it is a second *reading of Figure 1* - jg-rp's Rust parser behind
# Python bindings, written independently against the same grammar. Two
# readings that agree are evidence; one reading is an assertion.
#
# **0.1.4 accepts a quantifier of one digit only**, which is a defect in it and
# not a rule: `a{9}` is accepted, `a{10}` refused, and Figure 1 says
# `QuantExact = 1*%x30-39`. RFC 9485's own section 8 settles it by quoting
# `a{20,200000}` as an I-Regexp whose *cost* is a concern. libxml2 accepts
# both, so the two references disagree and the grammar decides.
# tools/oracle/iregexp_diff.py excludes the family by name and counts it;
# documentation/dialects.md section 10.5 has the reproduction. If a release
# past 0.1.4 fixes it, the exclusion is what to delete first.
iregexp	localhost/ghoti-regex-oracle-iregexp:0.1.4-lxml6.1.3	iregexp-check 0.1.4	Whether a pattern is an I-Regexp: the syntax half of RFC 9485

# **libxml2 answers the semantic half**: does this pattern match this string?
# RFC 9485 section 5.2 is what makes that possible - every I-Regexp *is* an XSD
# regexp and the mapping is the identity - so an XSD pattern facet runs the
# pattern, and a facet is anchored by definition, which is the whole-string
# Boolean section 4 borrows from XSD. `search()` has no reference here at all,
# and iregexp_diff.py says so rather than inventing one.
#
# **The pin is the libxml2 inside the lxml wheel**, not lxml: the wheel links
# its own statically, so `pip install lxml==6.1.3` pins an engine as well as a
# binding and the version field names both. That is also why the Dockerfile
# passes `--only-binary` - a source build would link this machine's libxml2 and
# the reference would quietly be something else.
#
# One image and two pins, which is the shape the four POSIX rows have for a
# different reason: those are four faces of one Debian userland, these are two
# wheels reached through one interpreter. What the two pins buy is the
# provenance line - a run says which of them answered.
libxml2	localhost/ghoti-regex-oracle-iregexp:0.1.4-lxml6.1.3	libxml2 2.14.6, lxml 6.1.3	XSD's Boolean semantics, which RFC 9485 section 4 adopts

